<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ayub Khan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1907">1907</a>–<a href="/years/1974">1974</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Muhammad Ayub Khan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ayub Khan was a Pakistani general who became commander-in-chief in 1951 and seized power in the coup of October 1958, ruling as president until 1969. He introduced a new constitution with indirect elections, promoted rapid industrial growth and moved the capital to the new city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamabad">Islamabad</a>. The war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> in 1965 and mass protests forced him to resign.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ayub-khan.jpg" alt="Ayub Khan" width="320" height="410" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Office of the President of Pakistan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ayub_Khan_portrait.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
